<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Visigoths</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Visigothic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Visigoths were a Gothic Germanic people who entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> in the late fourth century. They destroyed a Roman army and killed Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a> at Adrianople in 378 CE, and under Alaric they sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 410 CE. They later established a kingdom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, which lasted until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-conquest-of-hispania">Umayyad conquest of Hispania</a> in 711.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/visigoths.jpg" alt="Visigoths" width="320" height="262" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous (Visigothic)Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Visigothic_-_Pair_of_Eagle_Fibula_-_Walters_54421,_54422_-_Group.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Hispania the Alans received Lusitania and Carthaginiensis in the division of 411, and their king Addac ruled the strongest of the invading groups until the <b>Visigoths</b> destroyed his army in 418.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alans">Alans · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 486 Clovis defeated Syagrius, ruler of the last Roman enclave in northern Gaul, at Soissons, and Syagrius was later handed over by the <b>Visigoths</b> and executed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/franks">Franks · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…largest Roman province of Iberia, was governed from Tarraco for some five centuries, guarded the gold mines of the north-west with Rome's only Iberian legion, gave Latin literature Quintilian and Martial, and was the last part of Hispania to remain under Roman rule before the <b>Visigoths</b> took it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hispania-tarraconensis">Hispania Tarraconensis · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>He claimed Honoria, sister of Valentinian III, as his bride with half the Western Roman Empire as dowry, and in 451 entered Gaul, where Romans and <b>Visigoths</b> fought him at the Catalaunian Plains.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hunnic-empire">Hunnic Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Alfonso III (866–910), the court sponsored historical writing, notably the Chronicle of Alfonso III and the Chronicle of Albelda, which presented the Asturian kings as heirs of the <b>Visigoths</b> destined to recover the whole of Spain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-asturias">Kingdom of Asturias · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The kings stressed their descent from the <b>Visigoths</b>, and from the tenth century some styled themselves emperor (imperator), a claim to precedence over the other Christian rulers of Iberia: Alfonso VI called himself emperor of all Spain, and Alfonso VII was crowned emperor in León in 1135.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-leon">Kingdom of León · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
